g Increase of an Anammox UASB Reactor Generate Superior Anammox Granules.&#x201d; Water, Air, &amp; Soil Pollution 234, no. 3: 166. https://doi.org/10.1007/s11270&#x2010;023&#x2010;06157&#x2010;3.</Citation></Reference><Reference><Citation>Wu, P., J. Chen, V. K. Garlapati, et&#xa0;al. 2022. &#x201c;Novel Insights Into Anammox&#x2010;Based Processes: A Critical Review.&#x201d; Chemical Engineering Journal 444: 136534. https://doi.org/10.1016/j.cej.2022.136534.</Citation></Reference><Reference><Citation>Ya, T., M. Hu, J. Wang, et&#xa0;al. 2024. &#x201c;Autotrophic Biological Nitrogen Removal in Anammox System at Elevated Temperature: EPS Regulation and Metagenomic Analysis.&#x201d; Chemical Engineering Journal 498: 155838. https://doi.org/10.1016/j.cej.2024.155838.</Citation></Reference><Reference><Citation>Yang, L., X. Ge, C. Wan, F. Yu, and Y. Li. 2014. &#x201c;Progress and Perspectives in Converting Biogas to Transportation Fuels.&#x201d; Renewable and Sustainable Energy Reviews 40: 1133&#x2013;1152. https://doi.org/10.1016/j.rser.2014.08.008.</Citation></Reference><Reference><Citation>Zhang, L., W. Zhao, X. Ji, et&#xa0;al. 2024. &#x201c;Waste Iron Scraps Promote Anammox Bacteria to Resist Inorganic Carbon Limitation.&#x201d; Science of the Total Environment 912: 169042. https://doi.org/10.1016/j.scitotenv.2023.169042.</Citation></Reference><Reference><Citation>Zhang, Q., J. Zheng, L. Zhao, et&#xa0;al. 2023. &#x201c;Succession of Microbial Communities Reveals the Inevitability of Anammox Core in the Development of Anammox Processes.&#x201d; Bioresource Technology 371: 128645. https://doi.org/10.1016/j.biortech.2023.128645.</Citation></Reference><Reference><Citation>Zhang, W., D. Wang, and Y. Jin. 2018. &#x201c;Effects of Inorganic Carbon on the Nitrous Oxide Emissions and Microbial Diversity of an Anaerobic Ammonia Oxidation Reactor.&#x201d; Bioresource Technology 250: 124&#x2013;130. https://doi.org/10.1016/j.biortech.2017.11.027.</Citation></Reference><Reference><Citation>Zhou, J., T. Huo, J. Sun, et&#xa0;al. 2022. &#x201c;Response of Amino Acid Metabolism to Decreased Temperatures in Anammox Consortia: Strong, Efficient and Flexible.&#x201d; Bioresource Technology 352: 127099. https://doi.org/10.1016/j.biortech.2022.127099.</Citation></Reference><Reference><Citation>Zhou, Y., C. Wang, X. Xu, et&#xa0;al. 2023. &#x201c;Deciphering the Partial Denitrification Function of Companion Bacteria in Mixotrophic Anammox Systems Under Different Carbon/Nitrogen Ratios.&#x201d; Journal of Environmental Chemical Engineering 11, no. 6: 111232. https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jece.2023.111232.</Citation></Reference><Reference><Citation>Zuo, F., Q. Sui, D. Yu, S. Gui, K. Zhang, and Y. Wei. 2023. &#x201c;Effective Enrichment of Anaerobic Ammonia Oxidation Sludge With Feast&#x2010;Starvation Strategy: Activity, Sedimentation, Growth Kinetics, and Microbial Community.&#x201d; Bioresource Technology 388: 129730. https://doi.org/10.1016/j.biortech.2023.129730.</Citation></Reference></ReferenceList></PubmedData></PubmedArticle><PubmedArticle><MedlineCitation Status="MEDLINE" Owner="NLM" IndexingMethod="Automated"><PMID Version="1">41355421</PMID><DateCompleted><Year>2025</Year><Month>12</Month><Day>08</Day></DateCompleted><DateRevised><Year>2025</Year><Month>12</Month><Day>11</Day></DateRevised><Article PubModel="Print"><Journal><ISSN IssnType="Electronic">1554-7531</ISSN><JournalIssue CitedMedium="Internet"><Volume>97</Volume><Issue>12</Issue><PubDate><Year>2025</Year><Month>Dec</Month></PubDate></JournalIssue><Title>Water environment research : a research publication of the Water Environment Federation</Title><ISOAbbreviation>Water Environ Res</ISOAbbreviation></Journal><ArticleTitle>Steady-State Anaerobic Co-Digestion of Aqueous Condensate From Municipal Sludge Pyrolysis.</ArticleTitle><Pagination><StartPage>e70204</StartPage><MedlinePgn>e70204</MedlinePgn></Pagination><ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">e70204</ELocationID><ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1002/wer.70204</ELocationID><Abstract><AbstractText>Anaerobic digestion (AD) of condensate or aqueous pyrolysis liquid (APL) derived from municipal wastewater solids was successfully achieved both as a sole substrate and as a co-digestate with synthetic sludge, overcoming toxicity challenges previously associated with APL degradation. Key strategies that enhanced APL conversion to methane included optimizing the solids retention time (SRT) and organic loading rate (OLR) to mitigate APL toxicity, using an acclimated inoculum, and employing APL ozonation prior to digestion. Gas chromatography-mass spectrometry analysis confirmed APL constituents were removed in the process. Inoculum biomass from an industrial waste digester (IB) exhibited better performance in APL degradation compared to inoculum from a municipal digester (MB). APL ozonation enhanced methane production in IB-inoculated co-digesters, achieving 98% of the maximum stoichiometric methane. Microbial community analysis showed that hydrogenotrophic methanogens predominated in syntrophy with acetate oxidizing bacteria in IB-inoculated reactors, whereas both acetoclastic and hydrogenotrophic methanogens were present in MB-inoculated co-digesters. This study demonstrates that APL can be digested alone or as a co-substrate, emphasizing the importance of appropriate SRT, OLR, and inoculum selection. Co-digestion could be a viable strategy for wastewater resource recovery facilities that operate digesters for sludge treatment and may incorporate wastewater solids pyrolysis in the future.</AbstractText><CopyrightInformation>&#xa9; 2025 The Author(s). Water Environment Research published by Wiley Periodicals LLC on behalf of Water Environment Federation.</CopyrightInformation></Abstract><AuthorList CompleteYN="Y"><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Seyedi</LastName><ForeName>Saba</ForeName><Initials>S</Initials><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>Department of Civil, Construction and Environmental Engineering, Marquette University, Milwaukee, Wisconsin, USA.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>Jacobs Engineering Group Inc, Reston, Virginia, USA.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo></Author><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Venkiteshwaran</LastName><ForeName>Kaushik</ForeName><Initials>K</Initials><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>Department of Civil, Coastal, and Environmental Engineering, University of South Alabama, Mobile, Alabama, USA.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo></Author><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Oceguera</LastName><ForeName>Bethany</ForeName><Initials>B</Initials><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>Civil and Environmental Engineering and Earth Sciences, University of Notre Dame, Notre Dame, Indiana, USA.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo></Author><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Zitomer</LastName><ForeName>Daniel</ForeName><Initials>D</Initials><Identifier Source="ORCID">0000-0002-3686-1094</Identifier><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>Department of Civil, Construction and Environmental Engineering, Marquette University, Milwaukee, Wisconsin, USA.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo></Author></AuthorList><Language>eng</Language><GrantList CompleteYN="Y"><Grant><Agency>Milwaukee Metropolitan Sewerage District</Agency><Country/></Grant></GrantList><PublicationTypeList><PublicationType UI="D016428">Journal Article</PublicationType></PublicationTypeList></Article><MedlineJournalInfo><Country>United States</Country><MedlineTA>Water Environ Res</MedlineTA><NlmUniqueID>9886167</NlmUniqueID><ISSNLinking>1061-4303</ISSNLinking></MedlineJournalInfo><ChemicalList><Chemical><RegistryNumber>0</RegistryNumber><NameOfSubstance UI="D012722">Sewage</NameOfSubstance></Chemical><Chemical><RegistryNumber>OP0UW79H66</RegistryNumber><NameOfSubstance UI="D008697">Methane</NameOfSubstance></Chemical></ChemicalList><CitationSubset>IM</CitationSubset><MeshHeadingList><MeshHeading><DescriptorName UI="D012722" MajorTopicYN="Y">Sewage</DescriptorName><QualifierName UI="Q000737" MajorTopicYN="N">chemistry</QualifierName><QualifierName UI="Q000382" MajorTopicYN="N">microbiology</QualifierName></MeshHeading><MeshHeading><DescriptorName UI="D000693" MajorTopicYN="N">Anaerobiosis</DescriptorName></MeshHeading><MeshHeading><DescriptorName UI="D014865" MajorTopicYN="Y">Waste Disposal, Fluid</DescriptorName><QualifierName UI="Q000379" MajorTopicYN="N">methods</QualifierName></MeshHeading><MeshHeading><DescriptorName UI="D000077319" MajorTopicYN="Y">Pyrolysis</DescriptorName></MeshHeading><MeshHeading><DescriptorName UI="D008697" MajorTopicYN="N">Methane</DescriptorName><QualifierName UI="Q000378" MajorTopicYN="N">metabolism</QualifierName></MeshHeading><MeshHeading><DescriptorName UI="D019149" MajorTopicYN="N">Bioreactors</DescriptorName><QualifierName UI="Q000382" MajorTopicYN="N">microbiology</QualifierName></MeshHeading></MeshHeadingList><KeywordList Owner="NOTNLM"><Keyword MajorTopicYN="N">APL</Keyword><Keyword MajorTopicYN="N">methanogenesis</Keyword><Keyword MajorTopicYN="N">microbial community analysis</Keyword><Keyword MajorTopicYN="N">pre&#x2010;ozonation</Keyword><Keyword MajorTopicYN="N">syntrophic acetate oxidation</Keyword><Keyword MajorTopicYN="N">wastewater solids pyrolysis</Keyword></KeywordList><CoiStatement>The authors declare no conflicts of interest.</CoiStatement></MedlineCitation><PubmedData><History><PubMedPubDate PubStatus="revised"><Year>2025</Year><Month>10</Month><Day>15</Day></PubMedPubDate><PubMedPubDate PubStatus="received"><Year>2025</Year><Month>6</Month><Day>2</Day></PubMedPubDate><PubMedPubDate PubStatus="accepted"><Year>2025</Year><Month>10</Month><Day>29</Day></PubMedPubDate><PubMedPubDate PubStatus="medline"><Year>2025</Year><Month>12</Month><Day>8</Day><Hour>6</Hour><Minute>28</Minute></PubMedPubDate><PubMedPubDate PubStatus="pubmed"><Year>2025</Year><Month>12</Month><Day>8</Day><Hour>6</Hour><Minute>27</Minute></PubMedPubDate><PubMedPubDate PubStatus="entrez"><Year>2025</Year><Month>12</Month><Day>8</Day><Hour>3</Hour><Minu